p.src:
Suppose the value of a successful financing round is $A_{\mathrm{growth}}$, but the probability that the courtship produces a useful deal is $p_{\mathrm{deal}}$. The expected process value is then $\begin{aligned}
V_{\mathrm{process}}
&=
p_{\mathrm{deal}}A_{\mathrm{growth}}
-
C_{\mathrm{attention}} .\end{aligned}$ The process is worth continuing only while $\begin{aligned}
p_{\mathrm{deal}}A_{\mathrm{growth}}
&>
C_{\mathrm{attention}} .\end{aligned}$ This is the simplest way to capture the lecture’s reversal. As a courtship drags on, the expected value can move in two directions at once: $\begin{aligned}
p_{\mathrm{deal}} &\downarrow,\\
C_{\mathrm{attention}} &\uparrow.\end{aligned}$ The founders may begin with the thought that capital equals speed. But after enough meetings and frustration, the actual process can look like $\begin{aligned}
p_{\mathrm{deal}}A_{\mathrm{growth}}
-
C_{\mathrm{attention}}
&< 0 .\end{aligned}$ At that point, a rejection can preserve something valuable. It can stop a negative process before it consumes more of the founders’ operating attention.

